WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.628
On a big night, friends come to help in the kitchen.

2
00:00:05.628 --> 00:00:10.138
There's the counter, with one cutting board for each pair of hands that can chop.

3
00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:14.871
And there's the bench by the front door, where a helper waits to sign for deliveries.

4
00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:16.169
Chopping is work.

5
00:00:16.169 --> 00:00:18.502
Waiting at the door is only waiting.

6
00:00:18.502 --> 00:00:22.262
Now a helper stands at a cutting board, watching the door.

7
00:00:22.262 --> 00:00:24.985
Four helpers do that, and nobody can chop.

8
00:00:24.985 --> 00:00:26.216
That's not helping.

9
00:00:26.216 --> 00:00:28.291
That's guarding a cutting board.

10
00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:31.442
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:31.442 --> 00:00:34.842
Coroutines are cheap, and they pause instead of waiting.

12
00:00:34.842 --> 00:00:36.785
So why should the thread matter?

13
00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:42.300
Because only a suspending call lets go of its thread, the worker that runs the code.

14
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:49.009
A call that blocks, like an old database driver waiting for an answer, holds its thread until it returns.

15
00:00:49.009 --> 00:00:50.415
And nothing warns you.

16
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.565
Let's map it.

17
00:00:51.565 --> 00:00:53.347
The helpers are threads.

18
00:00:53.347 --> 00:01:03.669
The counter is Dispatchers.Default, a pool of threads for CPU work, one per core, the part of the processor that runs one thread at a time.

19
00:01:03.669 --> 00:01:05.005
4 cores, 4 boards.

20
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.736
The bench is Dispatchers.IO, for calls that block.

21
00:01:08.736 --> 00:01:10.928
Up to 64 can sit there at once.

22
00:01:10.928 --> 00:01:15.524
And when nothing names a place, launch and async use the counter.

23
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.008
The lesson's demo does this on purpose.

24
00:01:19.008 --> 00:01:28.729
On 4 cores, it starts 32 blocking calls of 200 milliseconds on Default, then asks Default for one tiny task, a microsecond of chopping.

25
00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:30.961
How long does it wait to start?

26
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.624
1,570 milliseconds.

27
00:01:35.624 --> 00:01:43.656
The 32 calls ran four at a time, in eight rounds, and the tiny task waited behind all of them.

28
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.019
A second and a half in line, to slice one cucumber.

29
00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:50.806
So the point is: blocking on Default doesn't crash anything.

30
00:01:50.806 --> 00:01:53.015
It just quietly stops the chopping.

31
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.445
Now send the same 32 calls to the bench.

32
00:01:56.445 --> 00:02:00.988
All 32 sit at once, and they finish in 255 milliseconds.

33
00:02:00.988 --> 00:02:03.097
The tiny task starts in 4.

34
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.525
Real work often needs both.

35
00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:12.039
To print a recipe card, we read its steps from a recipe book in a database, with a driver that blocks.

36
00:02:12.039 --> 00:02:15.105
Then we lay them out, which is pure calculation.

37
00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:20.514
withContext carries one step to the bench, and comes back to the counter with the answer.

38
00:02:20.514 --> 00:02:26.587
The caller pauses, the block runs on the bench, and the caller resumes on the counter, with the result.

39
00:02:26.733 --> 00:02:29.987
In Kotlin, printCard still reads top to bottom.

40
00:02:29.987 --> 00:02:32.272
Java names a pool at every stage.

41
00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:34.826
Each of these places is a dispatcher.

42
00:02:34.826 --> 00:02:40.245
It picks the thread that runs a coroutine, each time the coroutine starts or resumes.

43
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:42.654
Now a guess.

44
00:02:42.654 --> 00:02:46.069
Default and IO sound like two separate kitchens.

45
00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:50.125
So when the block hops to IO, a different thread runs it.

46
00:02:50.125 --> 00:02:50.552
Right?

47
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.220
Not in the lesson's run.

48
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:59.066
Both printed the same thread: DefaultDispatcher-worker-1.

49
00:02:59.066 --> 00:03:01.833
Default and IO share one pool of workers.

50
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.248
The same helper walks from her board to the bench, and the board is free for someone else.

51
00:03:07.248 --> 00:03:08.531
Same helper, new seat.

52
00:03:08.531 --> 00:03:14.479
The split gives you two budgets: blocking work can fill the bench, and every board still has a helper.

53
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.845
Here the picture breaks.

54
00:03:16.845 --> 00:03:21.660
A helper on the bench could chop between deliveries, but a blocked thread can't.

55
00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.174
And a call that suspends, like delay, gives its thread back wherever it is.

56
00:03:26.174 --> 00:03:28.461
So only blocking calls need the bench.

57
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.432
Three rules, then.

58
00:03:30.432 --> 00:03:36.386
Wrap each blocking call in withContext(Dispatchers.IO), inside the class that makes it.

59
00:03:36.386 --> 00:03:38.440
Keep long CPU work on Default.

60
00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.041
Don't wrap calls that already suspend.

61
00:03:41.041 --> 00:03:47.132
Nothing marks a call as blocking, so you mark it: chop at the counter, wait on the bench.

62
00:03:47.867 --> 00:03:51.650
One bench for every blocking call has its own problem.

63
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:56.905
The bakery's old ordering client blocks, and the bakery has 20 phone lines.

64
00:03:56.905 --> 00:04:00.758
When it slows down, 64 bakery calls can fill the bench.

65
00:04:00.758 --> 00:04:01.739
20 get a line.

66
00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:07.484
44 hold a seat, listening to hold music, and the recipe book's calls wait outside.

67
00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:10.817
So we put up a sign: bakery calls, 20 seats.

68
00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:16.544
In code, limitedParallelism(20, "bakery") makes a view of IO with its own limit.

69
00:04:16.544 --> 00:04:19.622
It owns no threads, and it adds to IO's 64.

70
00:04:19.622 --> 00:04:24.419
The bakery can't crowd out the others, and they can't crowd it out.

71
00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:26.295
One more thing.

72
00:04:26.295 --> 00:04:31.873
The dispatcher is written on a note clipped to each dish, with its name and its course.

73
00:04:31.873 --> 00:04:34.117
That note is the coroutine context.

74
00:04:34.117 --> 00:04:40.400
A ThreadLocal, a variable with one value per thread, is a note pinned to the cook's apron instead.

75
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:47.072
Write Ana on the apron, hop to Default, and the dish reads null: the note stayed with the first cook.

76
00:04:47.072 --> 00:04:50.981
asContextElement clips it to the dish, and Default reads Ana.

77
00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:53.633
So why does the thread matter?

78
00:04:53.633 --> 00:04:57.930
Because a blocking call holds it, and the counter has one board per core.

79
00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:04.111
The dispatcher picks the place, and withContext carries one step to the bench and brings the answer back.

80
00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:05.132
Quick check.

81
00:05:05.132 --> 00:05:11.626
On 4 cores, four coroutines on Default each make a 300 millisecond blocking database call.

82
00:05:11.626 --> 00:05:14.656
A fifth needs one millisecond of chopping.

83
00:05:14.656 --> 00:05:16.027
When does it start?

84
00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:25.174
After about 300 milliseconds, when the first call returns, because all four boards were held.

85
00:05:25.174 --> 00:05:27.997
Put the calls on IO, and it starts at once.
